  "reasoning": "The claim that \"lightning never strikes the same place twice\" is a well-known scientific myth. According to atmospheric scientists, the National Weather Service (NWS), and NOAA, lightning frequently strikes the exact same location multiple times. Tall, isolated, and conductive objects (such as skyscrapers, towers, and trees) are struck repeatedly; for example, the Empire State Building is struck by lightning an average of 25 times each year. Furthermore, a single lightning flash often consists of multiple strokes following the identical ionized channel in rapid succession.",
